had brought a new threat to the established crews. At the 1951 Worlds, the German pair of pilot " [:a {:href "/athletes/96104"} "Anderl Ostler"] " and brakeman " [:a {:href "/athletes/95820"} "Lorenz Nieberl"] " became champion at their first attempt, and emerged as new favorites for the Olympic title."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The German duo, who had originally been rivals before teaming up, set a blistering pace on the first of the four Olympic runs, and built a lead of 1¼ seconds. Although their next three runs were not as fast, they were still fastest on every run and finished nearly three seconds ahead of the second place Americans, with the veteran " [:a {:href "/athletes/85659"} "Fritz Feierabend"] " steering Switzerland into third. The German victory was achieved despite the use of a bob that had been manufactured in 1936."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The " [:a {:href "/results/267"} "1948"] " champion, " [:a {:href "/athletes/85501"} "Felix Endrich"] ", finished fourth in Oslo, but regained the World title the following year in Garmisch. A week later, he was killed instantly at the same track when his four man bob left the circuit and struck a tree."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"] [:th {} "Run #1"] [:th {} "Run #2"] [:th {} "Run #3"] [:th {} "Run #4"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/96104"} "Anderl Ostler"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/95820"} "Lorenz Nieberl"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]] [:td {} "5:24.54"] [:td {} "1:20.76 (1)"] [:td {} "1:21.64 (1)"] [:td {} "1:21.02 (1)"] [:td {} "1:21.12 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/83999"} "Stan Benham"] " / " [:a {:h